.SH NAME
.PP
auto-enable-autologout - Set idle time limit for known shells

\" Convention:
\" Underline anything that is typed verbatim - commands, etc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf 
.na
auto-enable-autologout minutes
.ad
.fi

.SH ARGUMENTS
.nf
.na
minutes     Maximum idle time before a user is logged out
.ad
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

The
.B auto-enable-autologout
command sets an auto-logout idle time limit for all users for as many
shells as possible.  This enhances security, especially in shared
spaces, where someone may walk away from a terminal running an
ssh session, su session, etc. and others have physical access to it.

Note: This only affects shell sessions, not graphical login sessions.
Hence, on modern Unix systems, which usually have a GUI, it is only
likely to protect remote sessions using ssh(1), su(1) sessiions,
or other sensitive sessions running in a terminal.  To protect
graphical login sessions, check the documentation for your
desktop environment regarding auto-logout and screen locking defaults.

Note: Each shell has its own syntax for setting limiting idle time.
Support for all shells will likely never be complete as new shells
are always being developed.  Please contribute patches for any
shells that are not yet covered.

.SH RETURN VALUES

0 on success, non-zero error codes otherwise.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.na
auto-enable-autologout
.ad
.fi

.SH FILES
.nf
.na
/etc/profile, /etc/csh.cshrc, /etc/bashrc, /etc/kshrc, /etc/zshrc,
and other shell-specific startup scripts
.ad
.fi

